window.SKIM_PAPERS["1606.08415"] = {"concepts": [{"id": "gelu-definition", "name": "GELU: the definition x*Phi(x)", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "The Gaussian Error Linear Unit is the activation GELU(x) = x * Phi(x), where Phi is the cumulative distribution function of the standard normal. It multiplies each input by the probability that a standard Gaussian draw falls at or below that input, instead of gating on the input's sign.", "explanation": "The paper defines GELU(x) = x * P(X <= x) = x * Phi(x) = x * 0.5 * [1 + erf(x / sqrt(2))], with X ~ N(0,1); the error function appears only because that is how a Gaussian CDF is usually computed. Because Phi(x) rises smoothly from 0 to 1, the multiplier is near 0 for very negative inputs, exactly 0.5 at x = 0, and near 1 for large positive inputs, so the function is asymptotically the identity on the right and asymptotically zero on the left. The paper's phrasing is that the unit scales x by how much greater it is than other inputs, in contrast to a ReLU, which gates x by its sign. The choice of the normal CDF is justified by the observation that neuron inputs tend to be roughly normally distributed, especially where batch normalization is used. The general form allows the CDF of N(mu, sigma^2) with mu and sigma as learnable hyperparameters, but the paper fixes mu = 0 and sigma = 1 throughout, so the activation introduces no new hyperparameters. Under those settings the function dips slightly below zero for moderately negative x (its minimum is around x близко to -0.75 in value terms, a small negative trough) before flattening toward zero, which makes it non-monotonic and non-convex. Substituting a different CDF gives related units: the logistic CDF yields the Sigmoid Linear Unit, SiLU(x) = x * sigma(x), which the paper names and reports as worse than GELU but usually better than ReLU and ELU.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Abstract and Section 2 (GELU Formulation), including the displayed definition of GELU(x); Figure 1 plots it against ReLU and ELU.", "ownerPaper": "1606.08415", "sources": {"paperId": "1606.08415", "note": "Abstract and Section 2 (GELU Formulation), including the displayed definition of GELU(x); Figure 1 plots it against ReLU and ELU.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "gelu-as-expected-stochastic-regularizer", "name": "GELU as the expectation of an input-dependent stochastic regularizer", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "The formula is derived by imagining a mask m ~ Bernoulli(Phi(x)) that stochastically zeroes or keeps a neuron's input depending on the input's own value, then taking the expectation of that random transformation. That expectation is exactly x*Phi(x), which is why the paper describes GELU as a nonlinearity with a probabilistic interpretation rather than a hand-picked curve.", "explanation": "The motivation combines three existing ideas: a ReLU deterministically multiplies the input by 0 or 1, dropout stochastically multiplies by 0, and zoneout stochastically multiplies by 1. The paper merges them by multiplying the input x by a mask m ~ Bernoulli(Phi(x)) — still a random 0/1 choice, but with the probability of keeping the input set by the input itself, so smaller x is more likely to be dropped. The paper calls this close to Adaptive Dropout, differing in that adaptive dropout is applied alongside a separate nonlinearity and uses a logistic rather than standard normal distribution. It reports that networks on MNIST and TIMIT could be trained competitively with this stochastic regularizer alone, with no nonlinearity at all. Since a deterministic function is usually wanted at inference and in most architectures, the paper takes the expectation of the random transformation: Phi(x) * (1 * x) + (1 - Phi(x)) * (0 * x) = x * Phi(x). This makes the activation and the regularizer two views of the same operation, which the paper contrasts with conventional stochastic regularizers that act irrespective of the input. It also reports that GELU still works well when ordinary dropout is applied on top, so the two do not conflict in practice.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 1 (Introduction), Section 2 (GELU Formulation) — the Bernoulli(Phi(x)) construction and the expectation derivation; revisited in Section 4 (Discussion).", "ownerPaper": "1606.08415", "sources": {"paperId": "1606.08415", "note": "Section 1 (Introduction), Section 2 (GELU Formulation) — the Bernoulli(Phi(x)) construction and the expectation derivation; revisited in Section 4 (Discussion).", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "gelu-tanh-approximation", "name": "The tanh and sigmoid approximations used in practice", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "Because the exact Gaussian CDF is comparatively slow to evaluate, the paper gives two cheap substitutes: 0.5*x*(1 + tanh[sqrt(2/pi)*(x + 0.044715*x^3)]) and x*sigma(1.702*x). The first is what the paper itself used in every experiment, and it is what most implementations mean when they say GELU.", "explanation": "The two approximations given are 0.5 * x * (1 + tanh[sqrt(2/pi) * (x + 0.044715 * x^3)]) and x * sigma(1.702 * x), where sigma is the logistic function. Both replace the erf evaluation with operations that are fast on standard hardware, and the paper frames the swap as trading exactness for feedforward speed. The constants matter: sqrt(2/pi) is approximately 0.7978, the cubic correction term 0.044715 * x^3 sharpens the fit in the transition region, and the 1.702 scaling in the sigmoid variant is what makes sigma(1.702 * x) track Phi(x). The paper attributes the tanh form to a published approximation of the area under the standard normal curve and states plainly that it used that form in every experiment reported. One practical warning is given: using a close approximation to the Gaussian CDF matters. A plain sigma(x) is also an approximation of a normal CDF, but the resulting x * sigma(x) — the SiLU — is a different, worse-performing activation in the paper's experiments, not a drop-in stand-in for GELU. The paper also advises training with a momentum-based optimizer when using GELU, as is standard for deep networks.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 2 (GELU Formulation), the two displayed approximations; expanded in Section 4 (Discussion) under the two practical tips.", "ownerPaper": "1606.08415", "sources": {"paperId": "1606.08415", "note": "Section 2 (GELU Formulation), the two displayed approximations; expanded in Section 4 (Discussion) under the two practical tips.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}, {"id": "gelu-vs-relu-and-elu", "name": "How GELU relates to ReLU and ELU", "tier": "minor", "parent": null, "summary": "GELU can be read as a smoothed ReLU: it becomes the ReLU in the limit sigma -> 0 with mu = 0, and the two agree asymptotically. Unlike ReLU and ELU, which are convex, monotonic and linear on the positive side, GELU is non-convex, non-monotonic and curved everywhere.", "explanation": "The paper writes ReLU as max(x, 0) = x * 1(x > 0) and GELU as x * Phi(x), so the difference is that the hard indicator is replaced by a smooth CDF — the same move that turned binary threshold units into sigmoid units. As sigma -> 0 with mu = 0 the CDF collapses to the indicator and GELU becomes the ReLU exactly; for large |x| the two functions agree asymptotically. Like ELU and unlike ReLU, GELU can output negative values. The paper also sketches a link to ELU: using the standard Cauchy CDF instead of the Gaussian gives a function asymptotically equal to the ELU with alpha = 1/pi on the negative side, and equal on the positive side after shifting the line down by 1/pi. The differences the paper stresses are that GELU is non-convex, non-monotonic and is not linear in the positive domain, so it has curvature at every point, whereas ReLU and ELU are convex, monotonic and linear for positive inputs. It suggests, hedging, that the extra curvature and non-monotonicity may make complicated functions easier to approximate. And it repeats the interpretive difference: ReLU gates on the sign of its input, GELU weights its input by how large it is relative to the input distribution, with the added property of being the expectation of a stochastic regularizer.", "prerequisites": [], "sectionIds": [], "floor": false, "citedFrom": null, "sourceNote": "Section 4 (Discussion), the paragraphs on relations to previous nonlinearities and on notable differences; Figure 1 shows the three curves overlaid.", "ownerPaper": "1606.08415", "sources": {"paperId": "1606.08415", "note": "Section 4 (Discussion), the paragraphs on relations to previous nonlinearities and on notable differences; Figure 1 shows the three curves overlaid.", "sections": [], "pages": []}}], "items": [], "edges": [], "themes": [], "pages": [], "narrative": null, "insights": null, "refs": {"accessed": []}};
